\section{Portfolio Weights and Turnover (0 bps)}

This section focuses on weight distributions and turnover across rebalancing frequencies and strategies. These allocation dynamics provide the foundation for the transaction cost analysis in Section~6.

\paragraph{Evidence and interpretation.} If proportional transaction costs scale with turnover, then weight dynamics are not merely descriptive: they determine the economic viability of a strategy. This section documents two empirically visible mechanisms. First, turnover falls mechanically as the rebalancing interval increases, because weights are adjusted less frequently. Second, differences across risk proxies tend to appear through cash usage and concentration patterns rather than through completely different sector exposures --- though, as discussed below, IQR is a partial exception on the turnover dimension specifically.

Beyond these headlines, two additional points are important for interpretation. First, ``low turnover'' does not necessarily mean ``small trades'': lower-frequency portfolios often exhibit lumpier reallocations (larger step changes at each rebalance) even if average turnover per rebalance is lower. Second, because estimation windows are defined as the immediately prior holding period, higher-frequency portfolios operate with shorter samples and can therefore exhibit more parameter noise, which mechanically increases trading as inputs fluctuate.

\subsection{Turnover Across Frequencies and Strategies}

Table~\ref{tab:turnover} reports turnover (mean rebalance-to-rebalance sum of absolute weight changes) across risk budgets and rebalancing schedules at 0 bps. The 10\% risk budget is highlighted as a representative ``medium'' case in the discussion below.

\textbf{Note on reconciliation with Table~1.} Table~\ref{tab:turnover} below reports the values from the dedicated turnover build (Table 2 in the original draft) and is treated as the source of truth for turnover figures throughout the paper. Five cells showed small discrepancies (at or below 0.001) against the terminal-performance table in the previous section (Table~1), with one larger discrepancy at the 1y/P95/10\% cell (0.579 in Table~1 vs.\ 0.572 here, a difference of 0.007). \textbf{Before submission, Table~1 in the frictionless-results section should be regenerated from the same pipeline run as Table~\ref{tab:turnover} so that every reported turnover figure in the paper traces to a single source of truth.} This is flagged explicitly here rather than silently patched, since a reproducibility discrepancy this specific (same cell, same strategy) is worth tracking down in the underlying code rather than assuming it is immaterial rounding.

Turnover is computed as the rebalance-to-rebalance sum of absolute non-cash weight changes (i.e., $\tau_k = \sum_{i \in S} |w_{k,i} - w_{k-1,i}|$), consistent with the transaction-cost model in Section~6. This treats the cash sleeve as a funding source/sink with no direct trading fee.

